.page ln 1 make hard links and symbolic links

# SYNOPSIS
*ln* [*-f*] _TARGET_ _LINKNAME_
*ln* [*-f*] _TARGET_ _DIRECTORY_
*ln* *-s* [*-f*] _TARGET_ _LINKNAME_
*ln* *-s* [*-f*] _TARGET_ _DIRECTORY_

# DESCRIPTION
`ln` gives a file a second name. Without `-s` it makes a hard link: _LINKNAME_ becomes another directory entry for the very same file, so a change made through either name shows through the other, and the file lives on until its last name is removed (`ls -l` and `stat` show the number of names). With `-s` it makes a symbolic link instead: a small file that holds the path _TARGET_.

If the second operand is an existing directory the link is created inside it, named after the target.

Hard links work inside one filesystem only (the root filesystem and `/tmp` are different ones), only for files and symbolic links and not for directories, and only on filesystems that support them: the in-memory ones do, FAT volumes do not. A symbolic link has none of these limits.

# OPTIONS
@ *-s*
    Make a symbolic link.
@ *-f*
    Remove an existing file called _LINKNAME_ first.

# EXAMPLES
    ln /tmp/report /tmp/report.bak
    ln -s /Hakurei/etc /tmp/etc-link
    ln -sf /bin/ls /tmp/dir

# EXIT STATUS
0, or 1 on failure (the message names the reason: another filesystem, a directory, an existing name).

# SEE ALSO
readlink(1), ls(1), realpath(1)
